============================================================
CORE CLAIM — independently reproduced on a scratch copy (attack 2, both
halves)
============================================================
Built a standalone repro under /tmp (not in the repo) mirroring
atomicWrite's staging+rename: symlink -> target with "original" content,
staged tmp file + os.Rename(tmp, link). Confirmed: Lstat on the destination
after the write shows a REGULAR file (symlink gone), target file left with
its old content untouched, i.e. the comment's central claim is TRUE for
atomicWrite itself.
Dangling-symlink case: pointed the link at a nonexistent path, ran the same
stage+rename; write succeeded, no crash, destination became a regular file
containing the new bytes, and the (never-existing) former target path still
does not exist. Consistent with the documented contract; no contradiction
found in either half of attack 2.

============================================================
ATTACK 3 (WORDING TRAP) — a write path THROUGH the link, bypassing Storage
==> REFUTATION FOUND, THIS IS WHY THE VERDICT IS FAIL
============================================================
The README's added sentence is unqualified: "The files you keep in `data/`
need to be real files, not symlinks: if one is a symlink, SimpleBudget will
replace it with a regular file the next time it writes to it, and the file
the link originally pointed to will stop being updated." It sits directly
under "### Step 3: Create the data directory" / `mkdir -p data`, i.e. it is
presented as a universal property of the `data/` directory, not scoped to
CSV/transaction files handled through Storage.

`grep -rn "os\.WriteFile" --include=*.go internal cmd | grep -v _test.go`
surfaces internal/config/config.go:175:

    func (c *Config) SaveUserSettings(settings map[string]interface{}) error {
        data, err := json.MarshalIndent(settings, "", "  ")
        ...
        return os.WriteFile(c.UserSettingsFile, data, 0644)
    }

`UserSettingsFile` defaults to `filepath.Join(wd, "data", "settings",
"user_settings.json")` (config.go:90) and is re-derived under
`BUDGET_DATA_DIR` at config.go:114 (`filepath.Join(dataDir, "settings",
"user_settings.json")`) — i.e. it is unambiguously inside the same `data/`
tree the README sentence describes, not some separate location. This write
does NOT go through internal/services/storage.Storage; it is a bare
`os.WriteFile` call, which uses O_CREATE|O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC and, unlike
atomicWrite's stage+rename, FOLLOWS a symlink at the destination rather than
replacing it.

Verified empirically (own Go program, /tmp scratch dir, not the repo):
    symlink -> target ("original")
    os.WriteFile(symlinkPath, []byte("NEWDATA"), 0644)
    Lstat(symlinkPath).Mode() -> Lrwxrwxrwx (STILL a symlink, not replaced)
    ReadFile(target) -> "NEWDATA" (target DID get updated, not "stop being
      updated")
    ReadFile(symlinkPath) -> "NEWDATA"

This is the exact opposite of both halves of the README's claim for this
file: the symlink is not replaced with a regular file, and the original
target does not stop being updated — it keeps being written through
indefinitely. One counterexample inside the documented directory falsifies
the blanket sentence as written.

Honest counter-consideration, disclosed rather than suppressed: grepping the
whole repo for call sites (`grep -rn "SaveUserSettings("` and
"LoadUserSettings(") turns up only the definitions, config_test.go, and
docs/api/internal/config.md — no handler, cmd, or MCP admin surface
currently calls either method. So in the CURRENT wiring, this path is dead
code and a user would not hit the contradiction through the running server
today. I weighed treating this as a mere "recorded observation" rather than
a refutation on that basis, but reject that reading: (a) the exported,
tested, documented method still performs a live write into `data/settings/`
the moment anything calls it, and the checklist's own attack-surface
instruction (#3) is worded exactly as "grep for os.WriteFile outside
storage/ touching DataDirectory... One counterexample -> the sentence
overclaims -> FAIL" with no reachability-from-a-handler qualifier; (b) the
README sentence is written as an unconditional promise about the directory,
not about "everything currently wired into a handler" — it should either be
scoped to storage-mediated writes or the config package's DataDirectory
write path should be fixed/excluded before the claim is published this
broadly.

============================================================
Attack 1 — other write/delete/backup paths (completeness), non-blocking
observations
============================================================
(a) createExclusive (uploads, internal/services/storage/storage.go:508+):
uses os.Link(tmpPath, path) — Link on an existing symlink destination fails
with EEXIST regardless of whether the link is live or dangling (does not
follow the link for the EEXIST check), so createExclusive never touches the
symlink's target and never publishes through it. Consistent with the
contract's "not honoured" framing; not misleading.
(b) internal/handlers/explorer's delete-original path in defaultImportDeps
(handlers.go ~555-567) uses os.Remove on a path OUTSIDE the data directory
(ImportDirectory), not inside it — out of scope for this contract.
(c) Storage.Remove / removeLocked (storage.go ~672-687) is a plain
os.Remove — POSIX unlink on a symlink removes the link itself, consistent
with "symlinks are not honoured," not misleading (the README sentence only
talks about writes, not deletes, so this doesn't need separate coverage;
recorded as an observation).
(d) backup Snapshot's buildZip (internal/services/backup/snapshot.go:167)
walks DataDir with filepath.Walk (which does not descend into a symlinked
directory but also does not skip a symlinked FILE) and does os.ReadFile on
every non-dir entry, including a symlink — this reads through the link and
archives the TARGET's bytes under the link's relative name, storing content
rather than the link itself. Restore's pruneExtras (restore.go ~278-330)
uses writer.Remove (os.Remove/unlink), so restoring over a re-created
symlink removes the link rather than the target. This asymmetry (backup
follows/dereferences; write and restore-prune do not) is a real,
independent completeness gap in the "symlinks are not honoured" framing
that the comment/README do not mention, but I record it as an OBSERVATION,
not a second refutation, because the added sentence's literal claim is
scoped to "writes" ("the next time it writes to it") and backup/restore are
not writes to that path in the sense described — a careful reader would not
be actively misled about atomicWrite's behavior by this omission the way
they would be by the false SaveUserSettings counterexample above.

============================================================
Conclusion
============================================================
Mechanical docs-only criteria (4, 5) PASS. The core atomicWrite claim (2)
independently reproduces TRUE, including the dangling-symlink edge case.
But attack 3's counterexample is real, reproducible, and inside the exact
directory the sentence is about: internal/config.go's SaveUserSettings
writes into data/settings/user_settings.json with a bare os.WriteFile that
writes THROUGH a symlink rather than replacing it, and leaves the original
target receiving updates rather than going stale — the precise inverse of
what the README now promises for "the files you keep in `data/`." The
sentence overclaims. FAIL per the checklist's own stated bar ("one
counterexample -> FAIL").
